    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"10 Second Challenge\"\nformat: html\n---\n::: tag-list\n[Residuals]{.tag} [Normal Distribution]{.tag} [Measurement Error]{.tag} [Bias vs. Variance]{.tag}\n:::\n\n## About\n\nStudents try to stop a timer as close as possible to exactly 10.00 seconds. Each attempt records a signed error (observed time minus target time), and after several attempts, the app summarises the mean error, best absolute error, and standard deviation of times. This is a simple but effective hook for **residuals**: every attempt's error is exactly analogous to a residual in a regression model — the gap between an observed value and a \"true\" target. With enough attempts pooled across the class, the errors tend to scatter in a roughly bell-shaped pattern around zero, giving a hands-on way to introduce the normal distribution of errors, and to separate two very different kinds of \"being wrong\": a **biased** timer (consistently early or late — a non-zero mean error) versus a **noisy** one (highly variable, but centred correctly — a large standard deviation with a mean error near zero).\n\n## App\n\nBelow is an example of what the app looks like when embeded into a Quarto document.\n\n```{=html}\n<iframe\n  src=\"Apps/ten_second_challenge.html\"\n  width=\"100%\"\n  height=\"700\"\n  style=\"border: none;\"\n></iframe>\n```\n\n## Download\n\nYou can download the app directly using the link below. This should open as a standalone app that can open on any device. You can also wrap this around a `=html` code chunk in your Quarto document to achieve a similar effect to what is seen above.\n\n<center>\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<button class=\"btn btn-success\" onclick=\"&#10;    async function downloadFile(event) {&#10;      event.preventDefault();&#10;      try {&#10;        const response = await fetch(&#39;data:text/html;base64,&#39;);&#10;        if (!response.ok) throw new Error(&#39;Network response failed&#39;);&#10;        const blob = await response.blob();&#10;        const blobUrl = window.URL.createObjectURL(blob);&#10;        const a = document.createElement(&#39;a&#39;);&#10;        a.href = blobUrl;&#10;        a.download = &#39;ten_second_challenge.html&#39;;&#10;        a.style.display = &#39;none&#39;;&#10;        document.body.appendChild(a);&#10;        a.dispatchEvent(new MouseEvent(&#39;click&#39;));&#10;        document.body.removeChild(a);&#10;        setTimeout(() =&gt; {&#10;          window.URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl);&#10;        }, 100);&#10;        return false;&#10;      } catch (error) {&#10;        console.error(&#39;Download failed:&#39;, error);&#10;        alert(&#39;Download failed. Please try again.&#39;);&#10;      }&#10;    }&#10;    downloadFile(event);&#10;  \"><i class=\"fa fa-save\"></i> 10-Second Challenge</button>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n</center>\n\n## General questions\n\n- After several attempts, were your errors mostly positive, mostly negative, or a mix of both? What would that pattern suggest about your internal sense of timing?\n- What's the difference between having a *mean error* far from zero versus having a large *standard deviation* of your times? Which one describes \"consistently wrong,\" and which describes \"unpredictable\"?\n- If your very first attempt happened to be exact (0.00s error), would that tell you much about how good you are at this task? Why or why not?\n- As a class, pool everyone's errors together and look at the shape of the distribution. Does it look roughly symmetric and bell-shaped around zero, or is it skewed one way?\n- In a regression model, a \"residual\" is the gap between what a model predicts and what actually happened. How is your error on this task similar to a residual? How is it different?\n- If someone was consistently early by about 0.5 seconds on every attempt, would practicing more make them more *accurate*, more *precise*, both, or neither? What would actually need to change?\n- Where else in real life do we see this same distinction between a **biased** measurement process and a **noisy** one (e.g. a scale that's always off by the same amount, versus one that gives a different reading every time you step on it)?",
